social media checklist 

The "Do's" Checklist
Prioritize Privacy Settings: Regularly audit your account security. Ensure that personal information like your phone number, home address, or current real-time location is only visible to people you trust.
Engage Authentically: Use social media to build genuine connections. Meaningful comments and sharing insightful content (like a new project or a tech breakthrough) add more value than mindless scrolling.
Fact-Check Before Sharing: In an era of rapid information, take ten seconds to verify a headline or a "viral" tip before hitting repost. High-quality sharing builds your credibility.
Maintain Professionalism: Treat your profile like a living resume. Even on casual platforms, assume a future employer or professor might see your posts. Consistency in your "digital voice" is key.
Practice Digital Wellness: Set boundaries for your screen time. Use social media as a tool for inspiration and networking rather than a primary source of validation.

The "Don'ts" Checklist
​Don't "Overshare" Sensitive Details: Avoid posting about internal company/school conflicts, financial status, or high-stress personal vents. These can have long-term professional consequences.
​Don't Engage with Trolls: It is tempting to argue, but "rage-baiting" rarely ends well. Protect your peace and your public image by ignoring or blocking toxic accounts.
​Don't Post Without Permission: Avoid uploading photos or videos of others without their consent. Respecting the privacy of your peers and colleagues is a fundamental rule of digital ethics.
​Don't Forget the "Delete" Button's Limits: Remember that once something is online, it can be screenshotted or archived. If you wouldn't want it on a billboard, don't post it.
​Don't Neglect Your Network: Social media is a two-way street. Don't just broadcast your own achievements; remember to support and celebrate the successes of those in your circle.

